---
title: Spaghetti aglio e olio
description: Lesson 5.2. Finishing pasta in the pan with its own cooking water.
servings: 2
time: 25 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, starch]
lesson: "5.2"
technique: finishing pasta in the pan
status: draft
---

> Technique: finishing pasta in the pan. The pasta comes out of the
> water early and cooks its last two minutes in the sauce, with some
> of its cooking water. Here the sauce is only oil, garlic and that
> water, so there is nowhere for a mistake to hide.

Bring @water{2%litres} to a boil in a #large pot{} with @salt{10%g}. Cook @spaghetti{200%g} for 2 minutes less than the time on the packet.

> Why so little water: you want the cooking water starchy, and less
> water means more starch in each spoonful. Pasta does not need a full
> pot.
-- source: Molly Birnbaum, "12 Common Cooking Myths, Debunked", America's Test Kitchen, 2019-05-30, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1706-12-common-cooking-myths-debunked ("You don't actually need a full pot")

While the pasta cooks, put @olive oil{4%tbsp} and @garlic{3%cloves}(thinly sliced) in a cold #large frying pan{} over low heat. Cook for ~{5%minutes}, until the garlic is soft and pale gold, no darker. Stir in @chilli flakes{1/4%tsp} and take the pan off the heat.

Scoop out a cupful of the pasta water, then drain the pasta. Add the pasta to the frying pan with 6 tablespoons of the pasta water.

Set the pan over medium-high heat and toss or stir hard for ~{2%minutes}, until the liquid has thickened, turned cloudy and clings to the pasta, with no pool of oil left in the pan. Add more pasta water if it dries out.

> Why toss hard: oil and water only stay mixed if the oil is broken
> into small droplets, and that takes force.
-- source: Kate Bernot, "The Best Way to Whisk, According to Science", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-01-01, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/3417-the-best-way-to-whisk-according-to-science

> Science: the cooking water carries starch that has come off the
> pasta. Heated in water, starch granules swell and release amylose, a
> linear chain that raises the viscosity of the liquid, so pasta water
> is thicker than plain water and thickens further as it boils down in
> the pan. Whether starch also acts as an emulsifier is not settled:
> the one physics study of a pasta sauce says only that starch "seems
> to stabilize" the mixture. Look up: starch gelatinization, amylose,
> viscosity, emulsion.
-- source: Bashir and Aggarwal, "Physicochemical, structural and functional properties of native and irradiated starch: a review", Journal of Food Science and Technology 56(2):513-523, 2019, https://doi.org/10.1007/s13197-018-3530-2 ; Bartolucci et al., "Phase behavior of Cacio e Pepe sauce", Physics of Fluids 37(4):044122, 2025, https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.00536 (a study of a cheese sauce, made with water from cooking 100 g of pasta in 1 litre)

Stir in @parsley{2%tbsp}(finely chopped) and serve at once.

> Next time, without the recipe: cook the pasta in less water than you
> think, take it out two minutes early, and finish it in the sauce
> with a splash of its water, tossing until the sauce clings.
